---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/electrical-trades/foundational/electrical-safety-and-theory">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Electrical Trades, and it deliberately teaches two texts at
    once: the safety procedure that keeps electricians alive and the circuit theory that makes
    their calculations work. You need nothing from earlier lessons — no physics, no workshop
    time. Every symbol is explained the moment it appears, and the arithmetic never goes beyond
    multiplying and dividing.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state Ohm's law and the power law, use them on a real appliance, and say what each quantity means;</li>
    <li>explain why series and parallel circuits behave differently, and why buildings are wired in parallel;</li>
    <li>describe what electric current does to a human body, and which numbers decide the outcome;</li>
    <li>use a multimeter and clamp meter safely, and explain the "test, then prove the test" ritual;</li>
    <li>carry out the safe-work procedure — de-energise, lock, tag, test, prove — and say what each step defeats;</li>
    <li>run a voltage-drop calculation and explain why the wiring rules limit it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One idea frames the whole page: the theory tells you what the current is doing; the safety
    procedure tells you what the current will do to you. The trade teaches them together
    because an electrician who knows only one is either a hazard or a bookkeeper.
  </p>

  <h2>The hazard that gives no warning</h2>
  <p>
    Most materials a tradesperson works with announce their state. A hot exhaust pipe hisses
    and shimmers; a rotten beam flexes and creaks; a gas leak smells. A conductor carrying a
    lethal voltage looks exactly like a dead one — no hiss, no glow, no smell. Electricity is
    silent, invisible and instantaneous in its consequences, and it does not give second
    chances at belief.
  </p>
  <p>
    That absence of warning is why the trade's most important rule is a <em>procedure</em>
    rather than a perception: never trust appearances — always test, with instruments, in a
    fixed order. A panel that looks switched off is energised until proven dead, and proving
    it is a skill with its own tools and rituals, taught below. The stakes are not theoretical:
    OSHA's own studies consistently find electrocution among the leading causes of death in
    construction, accounting for roughly 3 per cent of worker fatalities in an average year —
    and the great majority of those accidents trace back to skipped procedure, not unknown
    physics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the magnitude — electrocution's share of the toll" widget="estimate" config={esOshaEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate before revealing: electrocution accounts for roughly 3 per cent of construction
    worker deaths in an average year — about one in thirty. Notice the widget's sharpest
    line: a hot pipe hisses, a rotten beam creaks, a gas leak smells — a lethal conductor
    looks exactly like a dead one, and most accidents trace to skipped procedure, not
    unknown physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Electricity threatens in three distinct ways — shock, arc flash and fire — and every
    safety rule you will meet maps onto one of them. The cards below give the map; the rest of
    the page walks through it slowly, theory first.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each killer with the response that defeats it" widget="match" config={esThreatMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each killer with its answer and read the map: shock is defeated by de-energising and
    lockout/tagout, arc flash by distance and protective equipment, fire by conductors and
    breakers sized so nothing overheats in service. Notice the middle card's claim — Ohm's
    law is the arithmetic underneath every defence, from breaker ratings to voltage-drop
    limits.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the killers, the procedure, the law, the protection" widget="equationStep" config={safetyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each card pairs a hazard with the response that defeats
    it — shock is answered by de-energising and lockout/tagout, arc flash by distance and
    protective equipment, fire by conductors and breakers sized so nothing overheats in normal
    service. Notice too that the physics card sits in the middle of the sequence: Ohm's law is
    not a separate school topic but the arithmetic underneath every defence, from breaker
    ratings to voltage-drop limits. The order of the cards is the order of this lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>Ohm's law: the trade's one law</h2>
  <p>
    Circuit theory rests on three quantities and one law that binds them. <strong>Voltage</strong>
    (V, measured in volts) is electrical pressure — the push that drives charge around a
    circuit. Think of water pressure in a pipe: more pressure, harder push. <strong>Current</strong>
    (I, in amps) is the flow rate of that charge — how much passes a point each second, like
    litres per minute in the pipe. <strong>Resistance</strong> (R, in ohms, written Ω) is the
    opposition to flow — the narrowness of the pipe.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ohm's law</strong> says voltage = current × resistance: <code>V = I × R</code>.
    It is a cause-and-effect statement, not merely an equation: push harder through the same
    resistance and more current flows; squeeze the same push through more resistance and less
    current flows. Rearranged to I = V ÷ R, it becomes the question every electrician asks
    silently before touching anything: if this voltage reached my body, how much current would
    it drive through me? That one rearrangement is the arithmetic behind every shock hazard on
    this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The law's sibling, the power law <code>P = V × I</code>, converts flow into heat and work.
    Worked example, from the ground up: a 2,300 W kettle on a 230 V supply draws 2,300 ÷ 230 =
    10 A, so its heating element must have a resistance of 230 ÷ 10 = 23 Ω. Every calculation
    in the trade — element sizing, breaker selection, transformer loading — is this same
    two-step arithmetic at larger scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> voltage pushes, current flows, resistance opposes, and one law
    ties the three together — including the rearrangement that predicts how much current a
    given voltage will drive through a human body. Next: how those quantities behave when a
    circuit has one path or many.
  </p>

  <h2>Series and parallel: the two arrangements</h2>
  <p>
    Circuits come in two arrangements, and every electrician must see instantly which one they
    are looking at. <strong>Series</strong>: one path. The current is identical everywhere,
    resistances add up, and any break kills the whole thing — old Christmas lights where one
    dead bulb darkened the whole string were series. <strong>Parallel</strong>: multiple
    paths. Each branch sees the full voltage, the branch currents add, and one dead branch
    does not disturb the others.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="series or parallel? file the behaviour" widget="sort-buckets" config={esArrangementBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File each behaviour and let the buckets reveal the two signatures: in series the current is
    identical everywhere and resistances add; in parallel every branch sees the full voltage
    and the branch currents add. Notice which bucket holds 'one dead bulb darkens the
    string' — the single-path arrangement — and that total current, not branch current, is
    what heats the supply cables.
  </p>
  <p>
    Building wiring is parallel for exactly that reason: every outlet sees full voltage, and
    switching off the kettle does not darken the kitchen. But parallel wiring has a price the
    trade respects: every branch added draws more <em>total</em> current from the supply, and
    it is the total current that heats the supply cables. That leads to the most important
    sentence in practical electricity: <strong>resistance makes heat</strong>, at a rate of
    <code>P = I²R</code> — current <em>squared</em>. Double the current through a poor
    connection and the heat there quadruples. This is why loose terminals smoulder, why
    aluminium wiring's historical troubles were connection troubles, and why every ampacity
    table in the codebook exists to keep conductor heat inside what its insulation can
    tolerate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "voltage is what kills." The tradesman's saying "it's the
    current, not the voltage, that kills" holds an element of truth — current through the
    chest is what disrupts the heart — but taken literally it misleads. Ohm's law links the
    two: current = voltage ÷ body resistance. Higher voltage drives more current through the
    same body, which is exactly why DANGER–HIGH VOLTAGE signs exist. The useful reading is
    practical, not tribal: it is current you must keep out of your body, and voltage together
    with your skin's resistance decides how much gets in. Wet skin can drop to around 1,000 Ω
    against tens of thousands when dry — which is why bathrooms, rain-soaked job sites and
    sweaty hands are precisely where the trade slows down.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="bet the current before the arithmetic" widget="predict" config={esWetPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Lock in a bet before the arithmetic: a 230 V socket meeting damp hands drives on the order
    of 230 mA through the chest — orders of magnitude past anything the body shrugs off.
    Notice the wrong options' thousandfold spread, and that the honest arithmetic is current
    equals voltage divided by body resistance, with wet skin collapsing the denominator.
  </p>

  <h2>What current does to a body</h2>
  <p>
    A shock needs a complete path: electricity only flows in a circuit, so current must enter
    the body at one point and leave at another. Touch a live conductor while standing on the
    ground and the path runs hand to arm to chest to legs to earth. The path matters as much
    as the size of the current: the same milliamps are far more dangerous crossing the chest —
    through the heart — than passing from finger to elbow. Anything that shortens or insulates
    that path (dry gloves, rubber-soled boots standing on dry concrete, working with one hand)
    reduces the current, which is why old electricians' habits exist even when their reasons
    are rarely taught.
  </p>
  <p>
    The effects step up in stages, mapped out in the mid-twentieth-century experiments of the
    electrical engineer Charles Dalziel, whose shock-current table still appears in trades
    textbooks. Around 1 milliamp: barely a tingle. A few milliamps: a painful jolt, but you
    can still pull away. Around 10–30 mA: muscles seize — the "cannot let go" range, where
    the hand grips the very conductor it should release. Above roughly 30–50 mA across the
    chest, the heart's coordinated rhythm can collapse into useless quivering — ventricular
    fibrillation, the usually fatal outcome. Note the scale: these are thousandths of an amp,
    while the breaker protecting the circuit allows 20 <em>amps</em>. Ordinary protection
    watches a number hundreds of times too large to save a person, which is why special
    devices exist — later in this page.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="stack Dalziel's stages as the current climbs" widget="order" config={esDalzielOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Stack Dalziel's stages as the current climbs and watch the exits close: barely a tingle
    around 1 mA, a painful jolt you can still pull away from at a few milliamps, then the
    'cannot let go' range where the hand grips the conductor it should release. Notice the
    final card's phrase — across the chest: the path matters as much as the size, which is
    why dry gloves and one-handed habits exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a shock you can shrug off is harmless." It feels
    reasonable — pain seems like the natural measure of damage. But the dangerous currents act
    on muscles and on heart rhythm, not on pain nerves: a shock in the "cannot let go" range
    may hurt less than a hot stove while quietly stopping breathing or sending the heart into
    fibrillation. Pain is not the gauge; the current through the chest is. That is why the
    rule for any significant shock — especially an arm-to-arm or hand-to-foot path — is
    medical evaluation, even when it feels mild and the escape was quick.
  </p>

  <h2>Test instruments: making electricity visible</h2>
  <p>
    Because wire carries no visible state, the trade makes it visible with instruments — and
    using them safely is arguably the most valuable skill a beginning technician masters, for
    their own safety as much as their proficiency. The <strong>multimeter</strong> is the
    everyday knife: volts (AC and DC), amps, and ohms or continuity. Its uses are drilled as
    pairs, because connection direction matters: voltage is measured <em>across</em> a
    component (in parallel with it), current is measured <em>through</em> the circuit (in
    series — or, far more safely, with a clamp), and resistance is measured only on a
    de-energised circuit, because a live circuit both damages the meter and makes it lie.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three more instruments carry the safety weight. The <strong>clamp meter</strong> reads
    current inductively by closing its jaws around a single conductor — no breaking the
    circuit, no touching bare metal — which makes it the daily tool for load checks. The
    <strong>two-pole voltage tester</strong> is the simple, robust, hard-to-mislead instrument
    you test a circuit with before touching it, and the <strong>proving unit</strong> is the
    discipline's honesty device: before believing a "dead" reading, you prove the tester
    itself on a known live source. The <strong>insulation resistance tester</strong> (the
    "megger") applies hundreds of volts to check whether insulation on cables and motors is
    still sound. The meters themselves carry safety ratings — CAT II, CAT III, CAT IV — that
    certify the fault-energy environment they survive; a cheap CAT II meter on a service panel
    can fail explosively during a fault, so the rating is part of the tool, not an accessory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "my meter reads zero, so the circuit is dead." It is the
    most natural conclusion in the world — and the last one some experienced electricians ever
    made. A meter can read zero because its own battery is flat, its fuse is blown, its dial
    is on the wrong function, or it is probing the wrong pair of conductors. The procedure
    that catches every one of those failures is "test, then prove the test": verify the tester
    on a known live source immediately before <em>and</em> after the reading. A dead reading
    bracketed by two live proofs is the only evidence the trade accepts.
  </p>

  <h2>Safe work: the procedure, and what each step is for</h2>
  <p>
    Industry's answer to the invisible hazard is to make the circuit provably dead before
    hands go in — a condition engineers call a <strong>zero energy state</strong>: every
    source of harmful energy secured, not merely switched off. The working procedure is
    drilled until it is reflex: <strong>de-energise, lock, tag, test, prove</strong> — and
    only then touch. De-energise cuts the supply. Lock and tag stop anyone re-energising it
    while your hands are in the panel (the formal name is lockout/tagout, and it exists
    because "just a second" has killed). Test and prove establish that the circuit really is
    dead, with the meter discipline from the last section.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="walk the procedure — what does each step defeat?" widget="branch" config={esProcedureBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Branch the procedure and watch perception get replaced: the first cut exists because a live
    conductor gives no hiss, no glow, no smell — so energised is the professional's default
    assumption until testing says otherwise. Notice how each step has its own adversary,
    with lock and tag aimed at people rather than plasma, and how the walk ends: a zero
    energy state, every source of harmful energy secured, not merely switched off.
  </p>
  <p>
    The legal scaffolding around that procedure has two layers. OSHA's regulations give the
    backbone: employers must provide a workplace free of recognised hazards, and electrical
    work practices are codified in detail. NFPA 70E, the arc-flash standard, supplies the
    method for the hazard that shock rules cannot touch. An <strong>arc flash</strong> is a
    short circuit across air: conductor metal vaporises into an explosive plasma ball with
    temperatures near 19,000 °C, spraying molten copper, a pressure blast and a blinding
    flash. It can injure a worker standing well clear of the panel and is survived only by
    distance and equipment — so NFPA 70E requires hazard analysis, incident-energy labels on
    equipment, approach boundaries (how close you may stand) and arc-rated clothing and face
    protection matched to the energy on the label.
  </p>
  <p>
    Even good procedure cannot drive risk to zero, so the trade also trains its emergency
    response — starting with the rule that prevents a second victim: never grab a person who
    is still in contact with a live conductor. The current that holds them will take you too;
    cut the power or break contact with an insulating object first. Then ordinary first aid
    applies, and because electric shock can disturb heart rhythm hours afterwards, medical
    evaluation after any significant shock is standard, not optional.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "low voltage is safe, and my rubber-soled shoes protect me
    anyway." Household 230 V kills routinely: wet skin's resistance drops far enough that a
    socket can drive a lethal current. And consumer footwear is not electrical protective
    equipment — it is not tested, rated or guaranteed against any voltage. Real insulation
    comes from rated gloves, insulating mats, distance, and the dead-circuit procedure. The
    professional's default assumption inverts the amateur's: energised until proven dead.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> So far: the two arrangements divide the trade — series with one path where any break kills
    the string, parallel where branch currents add — and resistance makes heat at P = I²R,
    doubling the current to quadruple the heat at a loose terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>Safe circuit design: grounding and the devices that watch for faults</h2>
  <p>
    The safety features built into every installation extend the same logic to the moments
    when procedure fails. <strong>Grounding and bonding</strong> connect every metal part a
    person might touch to the earth, so no part can float to a dangerous voltage, and any
    fault current has a low-resistance path home — a path so good that the overcurrent device
    sees the fault as a short circuit and clears it in milliseconds. Breakers and fuses are
    sized to protect the <em>cable</em>, not the appliance: their job is to interrupt a fault
    before the wiring heats toward fire — the I²R logic once again.
  </p>
  <p>
    But a breaker cannot protect a person: a fault through a human body is at most a few
    hundred milliamps, far below the 20 A the breaker happily tolerates for hours. That gap is
    closed by the <strong>ground-fault circuit interrupter</strong> (GFCI, called an RCD in
    much of the world). It continuously compares the current flowing out against the current
    returning; a difference of about 5 mA — current leaking somewhere else, possibly through a
    person — trips it in as little as one-fortieth of a second, cutting the shock short of the
    dangerous range. OSHA requires GFCI protection for receptacles on construction sites,
    precisely because temporary wiring, wet ground and damaged cords are where people become
    part of circuits. Modern panels add <strong>AFCI</strong> protection, which recognises the
    erratic current signature of an arcing fault in damaged wiring — the fire hazard that
    trips no ordinary breaker at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the breaker protects people." It sounds right — the
    breaker trips, the danger stops. But breakers protect conductors: their trip current is
    hundreds of times larger than the currents that stop a heart, and a person can be
    electrocuted on a circuit whose breaker never trips. People are protected by GFCIs, by
    grounding, and above all by procedure. Each device guards a different number, and the
    numbers differ by a factor of hundreds — matching the right device to the right victim is
    the whole point of protection design.
  </p>

  <h2>From theory to the codebook: voltage drop</h2>
  <p>
    Every conductor resists, so every run of cable loses a little voltage along its length:
    drop = I × R, with R growing as the run gets longer and shrinking as the wire gets
    thicker. Copper's resistance works out to about 0.0175 Ω·mm² for each metre of length
    times each square millimetre of cross-section — the number the widget below uses. The
    consequences are not cosmetic. Lights dim, heaters underperform, and motors are worst of
    all: a motor starved of voltage draws <em>more</em> current to do the same work, heats
    itself further, and can burn out — a feedback loop. That is why wiring codes limit voltage
    drop, typically to 3–5 per cent of the supply voltage, and why the fix is always the same:
    bigger copper.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the voltage-drop arithmetic" widget="cloze" config={esDropCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Fill the arithmetic and follow the feedback loop: drop equals current times resistance,
    growing as the run gets longer and shrinking as the wire gets thicker — and a motor
    starved of voltage draws more current, heats itself further and can burn out. Notice the
    codebook consequence: caps near 3–5 per cent of the supply, and the fix is always bigger
    copper.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="voltage drop vs current, length and wire size" widget="sliderLab" config={dropCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> slide the length out to 100 m with a 20 A load on 4 mm²
    wire and the drop passes 17 V — over 7 per cent of a 230 V supply, well outside the
    guidance. Now step the wire size up to 10 mm² and watch the percentage collapse below 3.
    Drop grows in direct proportion to length and to current, and in inverse proportion to
    wire size: three sliders, one line of arithmetic, and a codebook consequence.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked circuit: one switch, one light</h2>
  <p>
    The theory condenses nicely in the smallest real installation. A 20 A breaker in the panel
    feeds a cable to a switch and onward to a light fitting; the switch interrupts only the
    <em>line</em> (hot) conductor, never the neutral, so that an off switch leaves the fitting
    with no voltage on it — the electrician's reason behind the wiring convention. The
    arithmetic checks the sizing: a 60 W lamp on 230 V draws 60 ÷ 230 ≈ 0.26 A, trivially
    inside the breaker — because the breaker's job is not to track the lamp but to protect the
    1.5 mm² cable, whose ampacity (roughly 15–20 A depending on installation method) sets the
    limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same circuit teaches fault-finding, which is Ohm's law read backwards. Switch on, lamp
    dark: meter across the fitting reads 230 V — the supply is fine, so the lamp itself is
    open-circuit (a burnt bulb). Reads zero at the fitting but full voltage at the switch —
    the break lies between them (a loose terminal or damaged conductor). Voltage appears where
    the circuit is complete and across the break; current flows only when the path is whole.
    Every diagnosis in the trade is this loop, scaled up: more switches, bigger cables, three
    phases — the same three quantities, the same one law, the same habit of measuring before
    believing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="one line of the fault-find is wrong — find it" widget="spot-error" config={esFaultSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Find the backwards line: current flows only when the path is whole — it is voltage that
    appears across the break, which is how a meter locates the fault. Notice the method's
    scale claim: more switches, bigger cables, three phases — the same three quantities, the
    same one law, and the habit of measuring before believing.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A 2,300 W kettle runs on a 230 V supply. How much current does it draw, and what is its element's resistance?</li>
    <li>Why is building wiring parallel rather than series? Name the everyday symptom the alternative would cause.</li>
    <li>A colleague says: "the meter reads zero — safe to work on." What must happen before anyone touches the circuit?</li>
    <li>The breaker is rated 20 A. Roughly what current through the chest can be fatal — and why does the breaker not help?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) I = P ÷ V = 2,300 ÷ 230 = 10 A, then R = V ÷ I = 230 ÷ 10
    = 23 Ω. (2) Parallel wiring gives every outlet the full voltage, and one dead branch does
    not darken the rest; series wiring would mean one failed bulb or switch breaking
    everything downstream — the old Christmas-light failure. (3) Nothing yet — a zero reading
    is not evidence. Prove the tester on a known live source immediately before and after the
    reading; a dead meter battery has killed electricians who skipped the proof. Then the
    circuit is locked and tagged so it stays dead. (4) Roughly 30–50 mA — thousandths of an
    amp — can trigger fibrillation; the breaker tolerates 20 A to protect the cable, hundreds
    of times larger, so people are protected by GFCIs and procedure, not by the breaker.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ohm's law V = I × R and the power law P = V × I are the trade's entire arithmetic; rearranged to I = V ÷ R they answer the shock-hazard question before every job.</li>
    <li>Series: one path, one break kills everything. Parallel: many paths, full voltage to each — buildings are wired in parallel.</li>
    <li>Resistance makes heat at P = I²R: doubling the current quadruples the heat. Loose connections smoulder; ampacity tables exist for this reason.</li>
    <li>Current through the chest is what kills: around 10–30 mA seizes muscles, above roughly 30–50 mA the heart can fibrillate — while a breaker tolerates 20,000 mA for the cable's sake.</li>
    <li>A "dead" meter reading only counts after the tester is proven on a known live source — test, then prove the test.</li>
    <li>De-energise, lock, tag, test, prove: the procedure that establishes a zero energy state. Lockout/tagout defeats re-energising; NFPA 70E governs arc-flash exposure.</li>
    <li>Breakers and fuses protect cables; GFCIs trip on about 5 mA of imbalance and protect people; grounding gives faults a low-resistance path home.</li>
    <li>Voltage drop = I × R grows with length and current, shrinks with wire size; codes cap it near 3–5 per cent because starved motors draw more current and cook themselves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This page is the licence to learn the rest of the trade. The next lessons put numbers on
    real work: cable sizing and conduit fill, wiring methods, motor circuits — each an
    application of Ohm's law to a table in the codebook. The safety procedure returns in every
    lesson too, because it is not a topic to finish but a habit to keep: every panel you ever
    open begins with de-energise, lock, tag, test, prove.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Alternating current adds its own subtleties.</strong> AC's reversals are what make
    transformers possible — voltage stepped up for transmission (slashing losses) and back
    down for use, which is why power grids run on AC at all. But coils and capacitors oppose
    current in ways that depend on frequency, through a broader quantity called
    <strong>impedance</strong>, of which simple resistance is one part; and
    <strong>power factor</strong> measures how much of the current actually delivers useful
    work — a number that matters on any industrial electricity bill. Three-phase supply, the
    commercial standard, delivers smoother power and simpler motors; single-phase remains the
    residential norm. The apprentice meets these as "beyond the basics"; the licensed
    electrician eventually lives in them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Arc-flash analysis is quantitative engineering.</strong> The incident energy a
    worker is exposed to (measured in calories per square centimetre) is calculated from the
    available fault current, the clearing time of the protective device and the working
    distance — which is why breaker settings matter for safety and not merely convenience, and
    why the label on a panel is the summary of a study rather than a decoration. The
    profession's safety hierarchy keeps pointing one direction: eliminate the hazard first
    (work de-energised as the default, energised work as a documented exception), engineer the
    exposure down second, dress for the arc last. It is the same hierarchy every trade learns,
    stated in the only language this hazard speaks: volts, amps, ohms — and the arithmetic
    between them.
  </p>

